(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Monoclonal antibody-based malaria prophylaxis represents an emerging) Tj
T*
(intervention strategy. We evaluated safety and efficacy of L9LS in an area of intense) Tj
T*
(year-round transmission. Methods: Following safety/tolerability assessment in age-) Tj
T*
(stratified cohorts, 324 children aged 5-59 months in western Kenya were randomized to) Tj
T*
(receive two L9LS injections \(10-20 mg/kg at months 0 and 6; n=106\), single injection) Tj
T*
(\(n=108\), or placebo \(n=110\). Children underwent monthly blood-slide examination for 12) Tj
T*
(months. Results: In the two-dose group, 66% of children experienced at least one episode) Tj
T*
(of parasitaemia during follow-up versus 83% in placebo recipients \(protective efficacy) Tj
T*
(42.7%; 95% CI 22.5-57.7; p=0.0003\). Adverse events were rare and balanced between groups) Tj
T*
(\(grade 3 or worse in 1% of L9LS injections versus 1% of placebo\). No serious safety) Tj
T*
(signals emerged. Peak antibody levels correlated with parasite suppression. Conclusions:) Tj
T*
(Bi-annual L9LS dosing conferred modest but clinically relevant malaria protection in young) Tj
T*
(children, with an acceptable safety profile. Higher dosing strategies warrant exploration) Tj
T*
(to improve efficacy in this high-transmission setting.) Tj
